  "question": {
    "text": "The electric potential in a region is given as&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>–</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>b</mi></math>, where&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi></math> is distance from the origin,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo></math>and <em><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>b</mi></math></em> are constants. If the volume charge density in the region is given by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>a</mi><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>, then what is the value&nbsp;of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi></math>?"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "24",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><img src=\"assets\\st0133_afb7a08efb9b.jpeg\" /></p><p><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>b</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>-</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi></math></p><p>The small amount of flux&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>d</mi><mi>&#981;</mi></math>&nbsp;associated with the closed surface bounded by two spheres of radius&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></math>&nbsp;is</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>d</mi><mi>&#981;</mi><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></mfenced><mfenced><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>A</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>-</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>A</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>d</mi><mi>&#981;</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>E</mi><mi>A</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>d</mi><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#160;</mo></math>and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>d</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></math></p><p>Using Gauss&#39;s law</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#961;</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><msub><mi>&#949;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></math></p><p>&rArr; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#961;</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>24</mn><mi>a</mi><msub><mi>&#949;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>24</mn></math></p>"
  }
